lesson: 156
title: Planting trees along a road, with a tree at both ends
level: primary-3
stars: 0
explain: When trees stand in a line with one at each end, there is always one more tree than there are gaps.
explain: Number of gaps = length ÷ distance between trees; number of trees = gaps + 1.
explain: A road 20 m long with a tree every 5 m: 20 ÷ 5 = 4 gaps, so 4 + 1 = 5 trees.
vars: a=name g=3..30 d=2..10
let: L = g * d
need: L <= 300
answer: g + 1
exercise: Trees are planted along one side of a road {L} m long, one every {d} m, with a tree at each end. How many trees are planted?
exercise: Lamp posts stand every {d} m along a path {L} m long, with one at each end. How many lamp posts are there?
exercise: {a} puts a flag every {d} m along a {L}-metre track, with one flag at the start and one at the finish. How many flags does {a.he} use?
exercise: Along a fence {L} m long, posts are set {d} m apart, with a post at each end. How many posts are there?
exercise: A row of bushes {L} m long has a bush every {d} m, from one end to the other. How many bushes are in the row?
test: From one end of a {L}-metre street to the other, a bench stands every {d} m, and there is a bench at both ends. Count the benches.
test: How many poles are needed for a line {L} m long, with one pole every {d} m, starting and ending with a pole?
test: Every {d} m along a {L} m corridor there is a lamp; the first is at the start and the last is at the end. How many lamps are in the corridor?
exercise: How many trees are planted along a road in Ohio {L} m long if there is one every {d} m, including both ends?
exercise: Street lights stand {d} m apart along a street in Dublin {L} m long, with a light at each end. How many street lights are there?
exercise: {a} plants a sunflower every {d} m along a fence {L} m long, starting at one end and finishing at the other. How many sunflowers does {a.he} plant?
exercise: Along a beach path in Queensland {L} m long, a sign stands every {d} m, with signs at both ends. How many signs are there?
exercise: Fence posts are put in every {d} m along a line {L} m long on a farm in Ontario, from end to end. How many posts are put in?
exercise: A coach sets out cones every {d} m on a straight {L} m course, with the first cone at the start and the last at the finish. How many cones are there?
exercise: Flower pots stand {d} m apart in a row {L} m long in a park in London, with a pot at each end. How many pots are in the row?
exercise: A road is {L} m long, a tree stands every {d} m, and both ends have a tree. Count the trees.
test: A canal bank in Birmingham, {L} m long, has a lamp post at each end and one every {d} m along it. How many lamp posts are there?
test: Every {d} m along a straight {L} m wall, {a} paints a star, with a star at both corners. How many stars does {a.he} paint?
test: How many posts hold up a clothesline {L} m long if they stand {d} m apart and the line begins and ends at a post?
test: A bridge {L} m long in Auckland has a flag every {d} m from one end to the other, ends included. How many flags fly on the bridge?
test: On a straight {L} m running track in Texas, a marker is placed at the start, at the finish, and every {d} m in between. How many markers are there?
test: Trees stand {d} m apart in a straight line from one end of an avenue in Melbourne, {L} m long, to the other, with a tree at each end. How many trees stand in the avenue?
